UNIFIED THEMATIC CLASSIFICATION — POSITIVE AND NEGATIVE FEEDBACK

1. OBJECTIVE

This section defines the rules for identifying and classifying themes and sub-themes extracted from user feedback about the Le Comptoir Campus IT support service.

It applies to both positive and negative feedback.

The objective is to produce a stable, consistent, meaningful and statistically comparable classification system for Power BI, while preserving the richness of individual comments.

Fundamental principle: Themes and sub-themes describe the aspects of the service being evaluated. Sentiment determines whether each aspect is perceived positively or negatively.

The same themes and sub-themes must be used for both positive and negative feedback whenever applicable.

A sub-theme must represent a reusable analytical category, never an individual reformulation of a user’s comment.

This section supplements the existing global prompt. It must not replace or interfere with sentiment analysis, justifications, or any other existing requirements.

MANDATORY LANGUAGE RULE: All themes and sub-themes must be returned in English, using exactly the official labels defined below. Never translate, paraphrase, rename or dynamically generate category labels.

2. CLASSIFICATION PROCESS

For each comment:

1. Understand the actual meaning of the feedback rather than relying solely on keywords.
2. Identify the specific aspects of the service that are appreciated or criticized.
3. Distinguish actual evaluation criteria from factual events, symptoms, consequences and contextual details.
4. Determine the sentiment associated with each identified aspect.
5. Assign each aspect to the most appropriate existing theme and sub-theme.
6. Preserve individual circumstances and specific details in the existing justification fields.
7. Never create a new category merely because the user’s wording differs from previous comments.

A comment may contain both positive and negative aspects.

Each aspect must be classified according to its own sentiment, independently of the overall sentiment of the comment.

Do not interpret a purely factual statement as positive or negative unless the wording or context supports that interpretation.

3. OFFICIAL SHARED TAXONOMY

The following 12 themes and 61 sub-themes constitute the official classification reference.

All labels must be reproduced exactly as written.

THEME 1 — Reception and Service Accessibility

Scope: Interpersonal reception quality, accessibility of the service and reception conditions.

Authorized sub-themes:

* Reception Quality
* Physical Accessibility and Signage
* Reception Staff Availability
* Opening Hours and Access Arrangements
* Reception Environment and Comfort

Examples: Friendly or unfriendly reception, welcoming staff, accessibility issues, convenient opening hours, comfortable or inadequate facilities.

THEME 2 — Waiting Time and Queue Management

Scope: Waiting experienced by users before or during reception at the service desk.

Authorized sub-themes:

* Waiting Time
* Queue Length and Crowding
* Queue Organization and Prioritization

Examples: Short or excessive waiting times, efficient or overcrowded queues, appropriate or inappropriate prioritization.

Waiting time at the service desk must be distinguished from overall processing and resolution delays.

THEME 3 — Processing Time and Responsiveness

Scope: Responsiveness, processing speed and compliance with expected or announced deadlines.

Authorized sub-themes:

* Initial Response Time
* Diagnosis and Intervention Time
* Resolution Time
* Procurement and Delivery Time
* Adherence to Announced Deadlines

Examples: Immediate or delayed assistance, rapid or slow diagnosis, short or prolonged resolution, timely or late equipment delivery.

THEME 4 — User Support and Assistance

Scope: Quality of assistance, understanding of user needs, professional expertise and support throughout the service journey.

Authorized sub-themes:

* Assistance Quality and Effectiveness
* Listening and Needs Understanding
* Expertise and Professionalism
* User Guidance and Education
* Service Orientation and Continuity
* Repeated Visits and Interventions
* Proactivity and Solution Finding

Examples: Effective or insufficient support, attentive listening or misunderstanding, competent or inadequately trained staff, appropriate guidance, proactive assistance.

The mere occurrence of an intervention does not automatically constitute positive feedback.

THEME 5 — Issue Resolution and Intervention Quality

Scope: Effectiveness, completeness and sustainability of problem resolution.

Authorized sub-themes:

* Resolution Outcome
* Resolution Completeness
* Solution Sustainability
* Technical Intervention Quality
* Verification and Case Closure

Examples:

* Problem resolved / problem unresolved.
* Complete resolution / partial resolution.
* Sustainable solution / recurring problem.
* Successful intervention / ineffective intervention.
* Proper verification / premature closure.

Never assume that an issue was resolved simply because an intervention occurred.

Distinguish an issue that was never resolved from one that reappeared after a successful resolution.

THEME 6 — Communication and Follow-up

Scope: Quality, clarity, accuracy and continuity of information provided to users.

Authorized sub-themes:

* Information Accuracy and Availability
* Explanation Clarity
* Progress Updates and Follow-up
* Communication of Expected Timelines
* Notifications and Confirmations

Examples: Clear or confusing explanations, accurate or incorrect information, proactive updates or lack of follow-up, reliable or misleading timelines.

THEME 7 — Equipment, Accessories and Returns

Scope: IT equipment, peripherals, replacement devices, equipment preparation, delivery and return processes.

Authorized sub-themes:

* Equipment Reliability and Functionality
* Equipment Availability and Stock
* Accessory Completeness and Compatibility
* Replacement Equipment Suitability
* Equipment Condition and Cleanliness
* Equipment Preparation and Testing
* Equipment Delivery and Provisioning
* Equipment Return and Traceability
* Equipment Loss or Theft

Examples: Functional or defective equipment, available or unavailable devices, complete or missing accessories, suitable or unsuitable replacements, smooth or problematic returns.

Do not artificially create positive counterparts for exclusively negative events such as theft.

THEME 8 — Software, Workstations and Data

Scope: Applications, workstation environments, configurations, installations, migrations and data management.

Authorized sub-themes:

* Application Functionality
* Software Installation, Restoration and Updates
* Workstation Configuration and Performance
* Data Migration, Backup and Recovery
* Data Synchronization and Transfer
* Printing and Peripherals

Examples: Working or malfunctioning applications, successful or failed installations, correct or incorrect configurations, successful migrations, restored or lost data.

THEME 9 — Network and Connectivity

Scope: Network availability, connectivity and remote access infrastructure.

Authorized sub-themes:

* Internet, Wi-Fi and Local Network Connectivity
* VPN and Remote Access
* Network Configuration

Examples: Stable or unavailable network connections, functional or inaccessible VPN, successful or problematic network configuration.

THEME 10 — Accounts, Permissions and Badges

Scope: User identities, accounts, access permissions, authentication mechanisms and physical badges.

Authorized sub-themes:

* User Accounts and Passwords
* Access Rights and Permissions
* Multi-Factor Authentication and Smartcards
* Physical Access Badges
* Access Provisioning and Changes

Examples: Restored or blocked access, appropriate or insufficient permissions, working or non-functional smartcards, functional or defective badges.

Distinguish physical badges from IT authentication smartcards.

THEME 11 — Organization and Procedures

Scope: Internal service organization, operational processes, coordination and working methods.

Authorized sub-themes:

* Procedure Simplicity and Suitability
* Team Coordination and Escalation
* Resource Planning and Staffing
* Case History and Service Continuity
* Training, Instructions and Working Methods

Examples: Simple or cumbersome procedures, smooth or ineffective coordination, appropriate or insufficient resources, consistent or fragmented case management.

THEME 12 — Overall Appreciation

Scope: General feedback expressing satisfaction, dissatisfaction, appreciation or recommendation without enough specific evidence to identify a precise operational aspect.

Authorized sub-themes:

* General Satisfaction or Dissatisfaction
* General Appreciation and Thanks
* Service Recommendation and Return Intention

The last two sub-themes are reserved for explicitly positive feedback.

Examples:

“Excellent service.”

Theme: Overall Appreciation

Sub-theme: General Satisfaction or Dissatisfaction

Sentiment: Positive

“Disappointing service.”

Theme: Overall Appreciation

Sub-theme: General Satisfaction or Dissatisfaction

Sentiment: Negative

“Thank you to the whole team.”

Theme: Overall Appreciation

Sub-theme: General Appreciation and Thanks

Sentiment: Positive

“I would definitely recommend this service.”

Theme: Overall Appreciation

Sub-theme: Service Recommendation and Return Intention

Sentiment: Positive

CRITICAL RULE: Never infer specific operational strengths or weaknesses from generic feedback alone.

“Excellent service” does not prove that waiting time was short, technical staff were competent or the issue was successfully resolved.

When specific reasons for satisfaction or dissatisfaction are provided, prioritize the corresponding operational categories.

Do not automatically add a redundant Overall Appreciation category.

4. SEMANTIC CONSOLIDATION

Different expressions referring to the same service dimension must be grouped under the same existing sub-theme.

Never generate a separate category for each wording variation.

Example A — Waiting Time

The following expressions belong to:

Theme: Waiting Time and Queue Management

Sub-theme: Waiting Time

Expressions:

* No waiting time.
* Five-minute wait.
* Very short waiting time.
* Excessive waiting time.
* Forty-five-minute wait.
* Unbearably long waiting time.

The sentiment and justification must preserve the distinction between positive and negative experiences.

A duration alone does not determine sentiment.

Example B — Reception Quality

The following expressions belong to:

Theme: Reception and Service Accessibility

Sub-theme: Reception Quality

Expressions:

* Warm welcome.
* Friendly staff.
* Smiling personnel.
* Pleasant reception.
* Unfriendly staff.
* Lack of courtesy.

Example C — Issue Resolution

The following expressions belong to:

Theme: Issue Resolution and Intervention Quality

Sub-theme: Resolution Outcome

Expressions:

* Issue resolved.
* Problem successfully fixed.
* Solution provided.
* Problem unresolved.
* Issue not fixed.
* No solution obtained.

Example D — Technical Expertise

The following expressions belong to:

Theme: User Support and Assistance

Sub-theme: Expertise and Professionalism

Expressions:

* Competent technician.
* Strong technical expertise.
* Professional support.
* Lack of technical competence.
* Insufficient expertise.

Always choose categories based on semantic meaning, not literal vocabulary.

5. DISTINGUISHING CATEGORIES FROM JUSTIFICATIONS

Themes and sub-themes must remain standardized.

Specific details must be preserved in the existing justification fields.

Example:

“The technician was very patient and spent twenty minutes explaining the procedure.”

Positive classification:

Theme: User Support and Assistance

Sub-theme: User Guidance and Education

The justification should retain the technician’s patience, the time spent and the clarity of the guidance.

Do not create new sub-themes such as “Technician Patience” or “Twenty-Minute Explanation”.

6. MIXED FEEDBACK

A single comment may contain positive and negative aspects.

Example:

“The team was very friendly, but I waited 45 minutes and my issue is still unresolved.”

Positive classification:

* Reception and Service Accessibility → Reception Quality

Negative classifications:

* Waiting Time and Queue Management → Waiting Time
* Issue Resolution and Intervention Quality → Resolution Outcome

Do not apply the overall sentiment of the comment to every identified aspect.

Do not classify routine activities as positive achievements unless the user explicitly expresses appreciation or a successful outcome.

Examples of activities that are not automatically positive:

* Equipment replacement.
* Ticket creation.
* Appointment scheduling.
* Technical intervention.
* Equipment delivery.

7. AVOIDING OVER-CLASSIFICATION

Identify only genuinely distinct aspects of the feedback.

Do not assign multiple sub-themes merely to reformulate a single appreciation or complaint.

Example:

“The team was very efficient.”

Do not automatically assign all of the following:

* Assistance Quality and Effectiveness.
* Expertise and Professionalism.
* Resolution Outcome.
* Technical Intervention Quality.
* Resolution Time.

Choose only the sub-theme genuinely supported by the comment and its context.

If the appreciation is too vague to identify a specific operational aspect, use Overall Appreciation.

A comment should generally contain one to three distinct thematic classifications, but more may be assigned when the comment explicitly describes additional independent aspects.

Each theme/sub-theme combination must appear only once per comment and per sentiment.

The same sub-theme may appear under both positive and negative feedback only when the comment genuinely evaluates different situations in opposite ways.

8. CONTROLLED HANDLING OF UNCLASSIFIED TOPICS

The official taxonomy must cover the vast majority of user feedback.

The AI must never freely create new themes or sub-themes.

Exception A — Existing Theme, No Suitable Sub-theme

When an existing theme is appropriate but no authorized sub-theme adequately describes the issue, use the following reserved sub-theme:

Other Reason (Review Required)

This exceptional sub-theme is authorized under each of the 11 operational themes.

Use it only after carefully examining all existing sub-themes.

Preserve the issue description in the existing justification field.

When relevant, suggest a potential taxonomy improvement within the existing justification, without changing the official category label.

Exception B — No Suitable Theme

When no official theme adequately covers the feedback, use:

Theme: Other Topics (Review Required)

Sub-theme: Out of Taxonomy (Review Required)

This classification must remain exceptional.

Mandatory Exception Rules

* Never use an exception for synonyms.
* Never create an exception simply because a duration, device, application, location or person differs.
* Never use an exception for generic satisfaction or dissatisfaction when Overall Appreciation applies.
* Never create alternative exception labels.
* Never use an exception to avoid making a classification decision.
* Never invent a new theme or sub-theme directly from an individual comment.
* Do not force classification when the comment contains no identifiable positive or negative evaluation.

Exception categories must remain marginal and should only identify genuine gaps in the official taxonomy.

Any suggested category must be reviewed before being added to the official taxonomy.

9. POSITIVE AND NEGATIVE CONSISTENCY

The same theme and sub-theme labels must be used for both positive and negative feedback.

Examples:

* Short waiting time and excessive waiting time → Waiting Time.
* Successful resolution and unresolved issue → Resolution Outcome.
* Effective assistance and insufficient assistance → Assistance Quality and Effectiveness.
* Clear explanations and confusing explanations → Explanation Clarity.
* Functional equipment and defective equipment → Equipment Reliability and Functionality.
* Fast intervention and delayed intervention → Diagnosis and Intervention Time.
* Friendly reception and unfriendly reception → Reception Quality.
* Well-managed procedures and cumbersome procedures → Procedure Simplicity and Suitability.

Never create separate categories solely to express opposite sentiments.

Likewise, never invent a positive or negative counterpart when the feedback does not support one.

10. STATISTICAL CONSISTENCY

The classification must ensure that:

1. Comments describing the same service aspect are assigned the same category.
2. Differences in wording do not produce new statistical categories.
3. Categories remain stable over time.
4. Rare but meaningful issues remain identifiable.
5. Consequences are not systematically treated as additional independent themes.
6. Category selection is based on semantic meaning, not frequency.
7. Positive and negative results can be compared using the same thematic dimensions.
8. Broad generic feedback does not distort the measurement of specific operational strengths or weaknesses.

The classification must remain suitable for comparisons across periods, locations, services and user populations in Power BI.

11. PRESERVING EXISTING OUTPUT STRUCTURE

This section modifies only the thematic classification logic.

Strictly preserve the existing JSON output structure and property names.

In particular, preserve the existing structures:

* themesPositifs
* themesNegatifs
* nom
* sousThemes

Preserve all existing sentiment fields, justifications, analysis fields and other required information.

Do not introduce mandatory new JSON properties.

Return all theme and sub-theme values in English, using exactly the labels defined in this taxonomy.

Store positive and negative classifications in their corresponding existing structures.

Exceptional categories must contain the exact marker “Review Required”, allowing them to be easily identified and filtered in Power BI.

12. FINAL QUALITY CONTROL

Before returning the analysis, verify that:

1. Every theme belongs to the official taxonomy or matches the authorized exception label.
2. Every sub-theme belongs to its selected theme or is an authorized exception.
3. Every category label is written in English exactly as defined.
4. Each aspect is assigned the correct positive or negative sentiment.
5. Synonymous expressions are consolidated.
6. Redundant classifications have been eliminated.
7. Generic feedback has not been converted into unsupported operational conclusions.
8. Individual details are preserved in the existing justifications.
9. Exceptional categories are used only when absolutely necessary.
10. The existing JSON structure remains fully compatible with the global prompt and downstream processing.

FINAL PRIORITY: Produce stable, accurate, semantically meaningful and statistically comparable classifications for positive and negative feedback, while preserving the precision of individual user comments and preventing uncontrolled category proliferation.

Point important pour ton administrateur : comme nous changeons les libellés des thèmes et sous-thèmes, il faudra également retraiter les analyses déjà enregistrées dans Dataverse pour éviter de mélanger les anciennes catégories françaises avec les nouvelles catégories anglaises dans Power BI.

Les noms des propriétés JSON, eux, restent inchangés pour préserver le fonctionnement de tes flux Power Automate.
